From: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: AQM eviction: reuse pass 1's scoring in pass 2

qos-13's two-pass score-then-evict eviction re-derived the same result
twice on every trigger. Pass 1 (mtk_qdma_aqm_score_queue()) walks the
whole flow table, and for every entry bound to the triggered queue
(mtk_aqm_get_foe_qid()) calls mtk_foe_entry_get_stats() - which takes
the global ppe_lock BH spinlock - purely to rank it into a top-`batch`
set and compute a score cutoff. Pass 2 (mtk_qdma_aqm_unbind_queue())
then re-walked the *entire* flow table a second time, re-filtered by
queue, and called mtk_foe_entry_get_stats() a *second* time per
matching entry just to re-derive the identical score and compare it
against pass 1's cutoff before evicting.

This is not a rare-path cost: qos-13's own on-hardware measurement
(docs/netsys-qos-port-investigation.md SS29) saw 17 triggers in ~35 s
under ordinary household load, so the doubled ppe_lock acquisition and
doubled flow-table walk recur roughly every 2 s under real traffic.
qos-13's design note also already flagged the resulting behavior as
"approximate under score ties across the two passes" - a direct
symptom of re-deriving rather than reusing pass 1's result: a
differently-tied candidate can be picked between the two walks because
each walk sees slightly different byte/idle counters.

Fix: have mtk_qdma_aqm_score_queue() write its selected top-N
candidates (including each one's entry->hash, the stable per-bound-
entry PPE slot id already used elsewhere in this file as the
"unbound" sentinel when == 0xffff) directly into a caller-supplied
array instead of only returning a scalar cutoff. Pass 2 then becomes a
cheap scalar membership check (entry->hash against the <=64 saved
hashes) while walking the table: no mtk_aqm_get_foe_qid() call, no
mtk_foe_entry_get_stats() call, and no ppe_lock acquisition beyond
mtk_foe_entry_clear()'s own. This halves ppe_lock traffic on every
trigger and evicts exactly the entries pass 1 selected, eliminating
the "approximate ties" caveat entirely.

No hardware register access changes; this is a value-preserving
strength reduction in the eviction bookkeeping, not a behavioral
change to which flows are eligible for eviction. Trigger detection
(qos-12) and the batch/grace_ms cooldown (qos-06) are unchanged.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -1346,6 +1346,7 @@
 struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand {
 	bool	active;	/* idle == 0 at the time it was scored */
 	u64	bytes;
+	u16	hash;	/* entry->hash, to re-identify the same entry in pass 2 */
 };
 
 /* True if @a should be kept over @b when only one of the two can stay in
@@ -1360,18 +1361,22 @@
 	return a->bytes > b->bytes;
 }
 
-/* Pass 1: score every flow currently bound to @queue (recency via the PPE's
- * own idle-tick from mtk_foe_entry_get_stats(), the same call the existing
+/* Score every flow currently bound to @queue (recency via the PPE's own
+ * idle-tick from mtk_foe_entry_get_stats(), the same call the existing
  * flow_offload stats path already makes elsewhere - no new register access)
- * and keep the @max best candidates by mtk_qdma_aqm_cand_better(). Returns
- * the worst-kept candidate as the pass-2 cutoff, and the number actually
- * found (may be less than @max).
+ * and keep the @max best candidates by mtk_qdma_aqm_cand_better() in @top.
+ * Returns the number of candidates written to @top (may be less than @max).
+ *
+ * @top's entry->hash values are the pass-2 eviction set: the caller must
+ * evict exactly these entries, not re-derive the same ranking a second
+ * time. Re-scoring in pass 2 could disagree with pass 1 on tie-breaks
+ * (both counts change between the two walks) and would double the
+ * ppe_lock/mtk_foe_entry_get_stats() cost of every trigger for no benefit.
  */
 static unsigned int
 mtk_qdma_aqm_score_queue(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned int queue,
-			 unsigned int max, struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand *cutoff)
+			 unsigned int max, struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand *top)
 {
-	struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand top[MTK_QDMA_AQM_MAX_BATCH];
 	struct rhashtable_iter iter;
 	struct mtk_flow_entry *entry;
 	unsigned int n_top = 0;
@@ -1393,12 +1398,13 @@
 
 		/* mtk_foe_entry_get_stats() takes ppe_lock (BH spinlock);
 		 * safe under the RCU read lock rhashtable_walk holds, same
-		 * as the mtk_foe_entry_clear() call this replaces below.
+		 * as the mtk_foe_entry_clear() call in pass 2.
 		 */
 		mtk_foe_entry_get_stats(eth->ppe[entry->ppe_index], entry,
 					&idle);
 		cand.active = !idle;
 		cand.bytes  = entry->bytes;
+		cand.hash   = entry->hash;
 
 		if (n_top < max) {
 			top[n_top++] = cand;
@@ -1415,25 +1421,32 @@
 
 	rhashtable_walk_stop(&iter);
 	rhashtable_walk_exit(&iter);
-
-	worst = 0;
-	for (i = 1; i < n_top; i++)
-		if (mtk_qdma_aqm_cand_better(&top[worst], &top[i]))
-			worst = i;
-	if (n_top)
-		*cutoff = top[worst];
 	return n_top;
 }
 
-/* Pass 2: evict up to @max flows bound to @queue whose score meets pass 1's
- * cutoff (currently-active flows first, ranked by cumulative bytes), instead
- * of qos-06's original arbitrary rhashtable-walk-order eviction.
+static bool mtk_qdma_aqm_hash_selected(const struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand *top,
+				       unsigned int n_top, u16 hash)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < n_top; i++)
+		if (top[i].hash == hash)
+			return true;
+	return false;
+}
+
+/* Evict exactly the flows pass 1 selected for @queue (currently-active
+ * flows first, ranked by cumulative bytes), instead of qos-06's original
+ * arbitrary rhashtable-walk-order eviction. Pass 2 only needs to re-find
+ * each selected entry.hash while walking the table; it must not re-derive
+ * the queue/score, which would cost a second ppe_lock'd stats read per
+ * candidate for a result pass 1 already computed.
  */
 static unsigned int
 mtk_qdma_aqm_unbind_queue(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned int queue,
 			  unsigned int max)
 {
-	struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand cutoff = { };
+	struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand top[MTK_QDMA_AQM_MAX_BATCH];
 	struct rhashtable_iter iter;
 	struct mtk_flow_entry *entry;
 	unsigned int cleared = 0;
@@ -1443,32 +1456,20 @@
 		return 0;
 
 	max = min_t(unsigned int, max, MTK_QDMA_AQM_MAX_BATCH);
-	n_top = mtk_qdma_aqm_score_queue(eth, queue, max, &cutoff);
+	n_top = mtk_qdma_aqm_score_queue(eth, queue, max, top);
 	if (!n_top)
 		return 0;
 
 	rhashtable_walk_enter(&eth->flow_table, &iter);
 	rhashtable_walk_start(&iter);
-
-	while (cleared < max) {
-		struct mtk_qdma_aqm_cand cand;
-		int idle;
 
+	while (cleared < n_top) {
 		entry = rhashtable_walk_next(&iter);
 		if (!entry)
 			break;
 		if (IS_ERR(entry))
 			continue;
-		if (entry->hash == 0xffff)
-			continue;
-		if (mtk_aqm_get_foe_qid(eth, entry) != queue)
-			continue;
-
-		mtk_foe_entry_get_stats(eth->ppe[entry->ppe_index], entry,
-					&idle);
-		cand.active = !idle;
-		cand.bytes  = entry->bytes;
-		if (mtk_qdma_aqm_cand_better(&cutoff, &cand))
+		if (!mtk_qdma_aqm_hash_selected(top, n_top, entry->hash))
 			continue;
 
 		/* mtk_foe_entry_clear() takes ppe_lock (BH spinlock).
